window.KIT_INDEX=[{"u": "README.html", "t": "Learn GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "", "x": "GitHub Copilot in Visual Studio Code (VS Code) works on the files on your computer. You ask in plain words, and it reads your documents, writes new files and runs commands . It asks you before it runs a command that could change something. You can undo the edits it makes to your files. These trainings teach you to direct it. You don't need to write code. Start with Getting started; each training builds on the one before it."}, {"u": "README.html#the-trainings", "t": "Learn GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "The trainings", "x": "Getting started : make your first request, change Copilot's work and undo it, have it explain code, and give it instructions for a folder. Skills : save your instructions for a task you repeat, so Copilot follows them when you ask for that task. Custom agents : set Copilot up for one role, such as reviewer or planner, with its own instructions and tools . Connecting your tools : connect Copilot to another service, such as GitHub, so Copilot can get information from that service."}, {"u": "README.html#which-to-use-when", "t": "Learn GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "Which to use when", "x": "Most of the time, you just ask. When you keep repeating yourself, or Copilot needs something it doesn't have, you can set Copilot up ahead of time. Find your case below, and try the smallest option that covers it. When this happens Use Taught in You need something just this once Just the request Getting started You give the same rules in every request in a folder Custom instructions Getting started You explain a task, such as a weekly summary, step by step every time A skill Skills You describe the same role, such as reviewer or planner, chat after chat A custom agent Custom agents Copilot needs information from another service, such as GitHub A connection Connecting your tools Often more than one would work, and one job can use several. VS Code has other ways to set Copilot up, such as hooks and prompt files. These trainings don't cover them."}, {"u": "README.html#when-you-need-it", "t": "Learn GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "When you need it", "x": "Help : what to do when something goes wrong, what to ask Copilot when you're stuck, and common questions. Glossary : the words these trainings use."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html", "t": "Getting started", "s": "", "x": "In this training, you make your first request to GitHub Copilot in VS Code. You change its work and undo it, have it explain code, and give it instructions for a folder. You can then do the same with your own files."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html#what-you-will-be-able-to-do", "t": "Getting started", "s": "What you will be able to do", "x": "Have Copilot create files in VS Code, and approve the commands it asks to run when they match what you asked. Give Copilot a task that takes several actions, and change the result by asking again. Undo a change Copilot made. Have Copilot explain an application's code, and point you to the lines that do a particular job. Give Copilot custom instructions it follows with every request in a folder."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html#before-you-start", "t": "Getting started", "s": "Before you start", "x": "A Windows computer with VS Code installed and signed in to GitHub Copilot. If either isn't ready, see Getting set up . If a button or menu has a different name on your screen, trust your screen: VS Code changes its labels. Ask Copilot what it's called now."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html#the-path", "t": "Getting started", "s": "The path", "x": "Make your first request to Copilot : open the chat and have Copilot create a folder to practice in. Change Copilot's work, then undo it : have Copilot write meeting notes and an action list, change the list by asking again, and undo the change. Have Copilot explain code you didn't write : have Copilot build a small application, explain it, and point you to the lines that do a particular job. Give Copilot custom instructions for a folder : have Copilot save custom instructions for summaries, and see the next summary follow them."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html#after-the-path", "t": "Getting started", "s": "After the path", "x": "Once you finish the path, you can try the practice recipes . Each is an optional new task that uses what this training taught. Do any of them, in any order."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html", "t": "Make your first request to Copilot", "s": "", "x": "In this unit, you open the chat in VS Code, have GitHub Copilot create a folder to practice in, and open that folder."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#1-open-vs-code-and-the-chat", "t": "Make your first request to Copilot", "s": "1. Open VS Code and the chat", "x": "You type your requests to Copilot in the chat. Press the Windows key, type code , and press Enter. On the View menu, select Chat . Check: the chat is open, and a control at the bottom of the chat box says Agent . If the control doesn't say Agent, or the chat won't open With Agent selected, Copilot can run commands and create files, not only answer questions. In a narrow chat, the controls may show only icons. If no control says Agent , select the chat box, hold down Ctrl, and press the period key (.). The list of agents opens, with the selected agent marked. Select Agent . Leave the other controls as they are. If VS Code doesn't open, no chat appears, or the chat asks you to sign in, see Getting set up ."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#reading-a-permission-request", "t": "Make your first request to Copilot", "s": "Reading a permission request", "x": "Copilot doesn't ask before it creates or edits files in the folder you have open. Before it runs a command that could change something on your computer, it asks in the chat and waits. Look at what it's about to do. If it matches what you asked, select Allow . If it doesn't, select Skip . Skipping is always safe: nothing runs."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#2-have-copilot-create-a-practice-folder", "t": "Make your first request to Copilot", "s": "2. Have Copilot create a practice folder", "x": "Your first request: Copilot makes the folder you practice in. In the chat box, enter: Create a new, empty folder named copilot-practice. Put it in the folder Windows uses as my Documents folder, which may be under OneDrive. Change nothing else on my computer. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it looks for your Documents folder or creates copilot-practice there. In Windows File Explorer, select Documents on the left. Check: Documents has a copilot-practice folder, and it's empty. If you can't find the folder If Documents has no copilot-practice folder, tell Copilot: I don't see copilot-practice in Documents in File Explorer. Tell me where you created it. Create it in the Documents folder File Explorer shows, and remove the other one. Change nothing else. Copy"}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#3-open-the-practice-folder-in-vs-code", "t": "Make your first request to Copilot", "s": "3. Open the practice folder in VS Code", "x": "Copilot works on the files in the folder you open. On the File menu, select Open Folder . In Documents, select copilot-practice , and then Select Folder . If VS Code asks whether you trust the authors of the files, select Yes, I trust the authors . If a bar at the top says the folder is in Restricted Mode instead, select Manage , and then Trust . Check: the list on the left shows COPILOT-PRACTICE , with nothing in it yet. If the chat or file list looks different VS Code reopens with the folder, and the chat starts empty. If no control under the chat box says Agent now, select the chat box, hold down Ctrl, and press the period key (.). The list of agents opens, with the selected agent marked. Select Agent . If the list on the left is missing, open the View menu and select Explorer ."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#what-you-did", "t": "Make your first request to Copilot", "s": "What you did", "x": "You opened the chat, had Copilot create a practice folder, checked it in File Explorer, and opened it in VS Code. Screenshots: Visual Studio Code documentation, Microsoft, CC BY 3.0."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html", "t": "Change Copilot's work, then undo it", "s": "", "x": "Copilot can carry out a task that takes several actions, and you can change the result by asking again. In this unit, you give it one task, change the result with a follow-up , and undo the change. Start with the practice folder open and the chat set to Agent , as at the end of Make your first request to Copilot ."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#1-have-copilot-write-meeting-notes-and-an-action-list", "t": "Change Copilot's work, then undo it", "s": "1. Have Copilot write meeting notes and an action list", "x": "In the chat box, enter: In the copilot-practice folder, create a folder named notes. In it, write three short, made-up notes from a weekly team meeting. Each note lists what was decided, and the actions with an owner and a due date. Then create actions.md beside the notes folder: a bulleted list of every action, with its owner, its due date and the note it came from. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it matches what you asked. In the list on the left, right-click actions.md and select Open Preview . Check: notes holds three files, and actions.md lists actions, each with an owner, a due date and the note it came from. About the files Copilot made Your notes and actions are worded differently from anyone else's. A file ending in .md is a Markdown file: plain text with simple marks for headings and lists. Open Preview shows it formatted. The preview updates when the file changes."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#2-ask-copilot-to-turn-the-action-list-into-a-table", "t": "Change Copilot's work, then undo it", "s": "2. Ask Copilot to turn the action list into a table", "x": "A follow-up builds on the last request, so it can be short. Enter: Make the actions in actions.md a table, sorted by due date. Copy Check: the preview of actions.md shows a table, earliest due date first."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#3-undo-the-table", "t": "Change Copilot's work, then undo it", "s": "3. Undo the table", "x": "Restore Checkpoint undoes the edits Copilot made with a request, so it's safe to try things. In the chat, hover over your last request, the one about the table, and select Restore Checkpoint . Confirm that you want to restore it. Check: actions.md is a list again, as it was before the table. What undo does and doesn't take back Restoring a checkpoint puts the files back as they were before that request and removes the request from the chat. It can't undo a command Copilot already ran; that's why Copilot asks first."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#on-your-own-work", "t": "Change Copilot's work, then undo it", "s": "On your own work", "x": "You can do this with your own files. On the File menu, select Open Folder , and choose the folder. Then give Copilot a task in plain words: what to read, what to make, and where to put it. Word and PDF files, and getting back here Word and PDF files aren't plain text, so Copilot may ask to run a small command to read them. If it reads the file you named, allow it. To get back to the practice folder, on the File menu, select Open Recent ."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#what-you-did", "t": "Change Copilot's work, then undo it", "s": "What you did", "x": "You gave Copilot a task, changed the result with a follow-up, and undid the change. Screenshot: Visual Studio Code documentation, Microsoft, CC BY 3.0."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html", "t": "Have Copilot explain code you didn't write", "s": "", "x": "Copilot can explain code you didn't write: what an application does, which file does what, and where a particular thing happens. In this unit, Copilot builds a small practice application, and you ask it about the code. Start with the practice folder open and the chat set to Agent , as at the end of Make your first request to Copilot ."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#1-have-copilot-build-a-small-tip-calculator", "t": "Have Copilot explain code you didn't write", "s": "1. Have Copilot build a small tip calculator", "x": "You need an application to ask about, so Copilot makes one. In the chat box, enter: In the copilot-practice folder, create a folder named tip-calculator. In it, make a small web page that works out a tip from a bill and a percentage: index.html, style.css and script.js. Keep the code short and plain. Change nothing else. Copy In the list on the left, open tip-calculator . Check: tip-calculator holds index.html , style.css and script.js . Do I need to read the code? You don't need to read the code yourself. These three files are the usual parts of a web page: the page, how it looks, and what it does. Copilot writes the code fresh each time, so yours won't match anyone else's."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#2-ask-copilot-what-the-tip-calculator-does", "t": "Have Copilot explain code you didn't write", "s": "2. Ask Copilot what the tip calculator does", "x": "Start by asking, in plain words, what the application does. Enter: Explain the application in the tip-calculator folder for someone who doesn't write code. Say what it does, and what each file is for. Copy Check: the reply names all three files, each with what it's for."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#3-ask-copilot-which-lines-work-out-the-tip", "t": "Have Copilot explain code you didn't write", "s": "3. Ask Copilot which lines work out the tip", "x": "Ask for the file and the line numbers, and you can go straight to the code. Enter: Read the files in the tip-calculator folder. Tell me where the tip is worked out. Name the file and the line numbers, and quote those lines. Copy In the list on the left, select the file it names. It opens in the middle of the window, with line numbers down its left edge. Check: the lines it quoted are at the line numbers it named. If the lines don't match Tell it: Those lines aren't at the line numbers you gave. Look again, and quote only what's in the file. Copy"}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#on-your-own-work", "t": "Have Copilot explain code you didn't write", "s": "On your own work", "x": "The same questions work on a real application's code. On the File menu, select Open Folder , and choose the application's folder. If VS Code asks whether you trust the authors, select Yes, I trust the authors only when you trust where its code came from. Then ask what it does, and where something you care about happens."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#what-you-did", "t": "Have Copilot explain code you didn't write", "s": "What you did", "x": "You had Copilot build a small application, explain it in plain words, and point you to the lines that do a particular job."}, {"u": "getting-started/custom-instructions.html", "t": "Give Copilot custom instructions for a folder", "s": "", "x": "Custom instructions are notes Copilot reads before every request in a folder: what the work is, who reads it, and how you want it done. You stop repeating them. In this unit, you ask for a summary, have Copilot write down how you want summaries, and ask again. Start with the practice folder open. It holds the notes folder from Change Copilot's work, then undo it ."}, {"u": "getting-started/custom-instructions.html#1-ask-copilot-to-summarize-the-meeting-notes", "t": "Give Copilot custom instructions for a folder", "s": "1. Ask Copilot to summarize the meeting notes", "x": "You'll compare this answer with the next one. In the chat box, enter: Summarize the meeting notes in the notes folder of the copilot-practice folder. Copy Check: the chat shows a summary of the notes. Look at what comes first, and whether each item names the note it came from."}, {"u": "getting-started/custom-instructions.html#2-have-copilot-save-custom-instructions-for-summaries", "t": "Give Copilot custom instructions for a folder", "s": "2. Have Copilot save custom instructions for summaries", "x": "Enter: In the copilot-practice folder, create .github/copilot-instructions.md describing this work: - The notes folder holds a team's weekly meeting notes. - The people who read summaries of them are busy and not technical. - They only need what they must act on: lead with the actions, grouped by owner, then one line of decisions. - After each item, name the note it came from. If that file already exists, don't change it; tell me instead. Add or change no other file. Copy In the list on the left, open .github and select copilot-instructions.md . Check: the file holds the four points you asked for. Why the file name matters Copilot reads this file before every request in this folder. It must have exactly this name, in a folder named .github . Nothing is sent to GitHub."}, {"u": "getting-started/custom-instructions.html#3-ask-for-the-summary-again-and-compare", "t": "Give Copilot custom instructions for a folder", "s": "3. Ask for the summary again, and compare", "x": "A new chat starts without the first answer, so any difference comes from the instructions. At the top of the chat, select New Chat (+). Enter the same request as before: Summarize the meeting notes in the notes folder of the copilot-practice folder. Copy Check: the new summary leads with the actions, grouped by owner, then one line of decisions, and names the note behind each item. If the summary ignores the instructions Send: Follow the instructions in .github/copilot-instructions.md and summarize again. Copy"}, {"u": "getting-started/custom-instructions.html#4-have-copilot-delete-the-custom-instructions", "t": "Give Copilot custom instructions for a folder", "s": "4. Have Copilot delete the custom instructions", "x": "Left in place, these instructions shape every later answer in this folder, so remove them. Enter: Delete the .github folder in the copilot-practice folder, and nothing else. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it deletes only that folder. Check: the list on the left no longer shows .github ."}, {"u": "getting-started/custom-instructions.html#on-your-own-work", "t": "Give Copilot custom instructions for a folder", "s": "On your own work", "x": "Real custom instructions hold your context and standards: what the work is, who it's for, and how you want it written. In a folder of your own, ask Copilot to create .github/copilot-instructions.md holding yours. If the folder already has one, ask Copilot to add to it rather than replace it. Important: instructions saved in a folder apply only while that folder is open. For instructions that apply in every folder, ask Copilot to help you set up personal custom instructions."}, {"u": "getting-started/custom-instructions.html#what-you-did", "t": "Give Copilot custom instructions for a folder", "s": "What you did", "x": "You saved instructions once, saw Copilot follow them without being asked, then removed them."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/index.html", "t": "Practice recipes", "s": "", "x": "These recipes are optional practice for after you finish Getting started. Each is a new task that uses what the training taught. Do any of them, in any order. Copilot creates the practice files for you. Kind of work Recipe You end with An export from a system, as a CSV file Analyze an exported spreadsheet analysis.md , with counts and the oldest open tickets, and one figure you checked Documents someone handed you Find what is missing from documentation gaps.md , questions for the team that sent the documents A change request to approve Review a change request for go or no-go review.md , with a suggested go, no-go or ask for more information for each change request A folder that grew without a plan Organize a folder A sorted folder and moves.md Many files that need the same change Change many files at once Updated copies of the files, with the originals unchanged"}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/analyze-an-export.html", "t": "Analyze an exported spreadsheet", "s": "", "x": "You end with: analysis.md , with counts and the oldest open tickets from a CSV export, and one figure you checked yourself. You need: the copilot-practice folder open in VS Code. If another folder is open, on the File menu, select Open Recent , and then select copilot-practice . When: a system gives you a CSV export, such as a ticket list or an incident list, and you need counts or the oldest items."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/analyze-an-export.html#1-have-copilot-create-a-practice-export", "t": "Analyze an exported spreadsheet", "s": "1. Have Copilot create a practice export", "x": "A made-up export gives you tickets to count without using real data. If the control at the bottom of the chat box doesn't show Agent , select the control, and then select Agent . In the chat box, enter: In the copilot-practice folder, create a folder named export-practice. In it, create tickets.csv: 40 rows of made-up service desk tickets. Give it the columns Ticket, Opened, Category, Group and Status. Use five categories. Make 15 tickets Open and the rest Closed. Add or change no other file. Copy In the list on the left, expand export-practice , and select tickets.csv . Check: the first line holds the five column names, and each line after it is one ticket, with commas between the values. The last ticket is on line 41, so there are 40 tickets. If the last ticket is on another line, subtract one from that line number. That's how many tickets to expect below."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/analyze-an-export.html#2-ask-your-questions-and-ask-how-copilot-got-each-figure", "t": "Analyze an exported spreadsheet", "s": "2. Ask your questions, and ask how Copilot got each figure", "x": "Asking how it got each figure tells you what to check. Enter: Read tickets.csv in the export-practice folder of the copilot-practice folder. Tell me how many tickets it has, and how many are in each category. List the ten oldest open tickets, with their age in days as of today. For each figure, say which column and which filter you used. Don't change any file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it reads only tickets.csv . Check: the reply counts all the tickets, gives a count for each category, and names the column and filter behind each figure."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/analyze-an-export.html#3-check-one-figure-yourself", "t": "Analyze an exported spreadsheet", "s": "3. Check one figure yourself", "x": "Counting one figure yourself shows whether Copilot counted the way it said it did. Take the category with the fewest tickets in Copilot's answer. If two tie, take either. In tickets.csv , count that category's tickets yourself. Check: your count matches Copilot's. If it doesn't, enter: How did you count the category with the fewest tickets? List the rows you counted. Don't change any file. Copy Compare its list with the file. If Copilot's count was wrong, tell it which rows it got wrong, and ask it to correct its figures before you save them."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/analyze-an-export.html#4-save-the-answers", "t": "Analyze an exported spreadsheet", "s": "4. Save the answers", "x": "In the same chat, enter: In the export-practice folder, create analysis.md with the category counts and the ten oldest open tickets from your answer, and a one-paragraph summary. Add or change no other file. Copy In the list on the left, right-click analysis.md and select Open Preview . Check: the preview shows the counts and the ten oldest open tickets, and the category you counted has your count. To use your own export: save it as a CSV file in a folder, and open that folder. Send the same requests, except the one that creates practice files. Use your file's name, and leave out the practice folder names. If it does not work: Help ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/find-what-is-missing-from-documentation.html", "t": "Find what is missing from documentation", "s": "", "x": "You end with: gaps.md , the things a set of documents doesn't say, written as questions for the team that sent them. You need: the copilot-practice folder open in VS Code. If another folder is open, on the File menu, select Open Recent , and then select copilot-practice . When: a team hands you documentation for an application or a process, and you have to support or approve something based on it."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/find-what-is-missing-from-documentation.html#1-have-copilot-create-practice-documents", "t": "Find what is missing from documentation", "s": "1. Have Copilot create practice documents", "x": "Copilot leaves two things out of the documents on purpose, so you know what it should find. If the control at the bottom of the chat box doesn't show Agent , select the control, and then select Agent . In the chat box, enter: In the copilot-practice folder, create a folder named docs-practice. In it, write three short, made-up documents for an application named Order Tracker: an overview, a setup guide and a support guide. Leave out who to call when it fails, and how to restart it. Then create what-support-needs.md in the same folder: six things a support team must find in any application's documentation. Make who to call and how to restart the application two of the six. Add or change no other file. Copy Check: docs-practice holds the three documents and what-support-needs.md ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/find-what-is-missing-from-documentation.html#2-ask-where-each-thing-is-answered", "t": "Find what is missing from documentation", "s": "2. Ask where each thing is answered", "x": "A quote is an answer you can find in the document. \"Not found\" gives Copilot a way to report a gap instead of guessing. Enter: Read the three documents in the docs-practice folder of the copilot-practice folder. For each item in what-support-needs.md, quote the passage that answers it and name its document, or write \"not found\". Don't fill gaps from general knowledge. Don't change any file. Copy Check: who to call and how to restart Order Tracker are among the items \"not found\". Pick one item that has a quote. Open the document it names, and find the quote there. If who to call or how to restart Order Tracker has a quote, open the document it names and read it. A quote such as \"Contact: to be confirmed\" doesn't answer the item. If that's what you find, tell Copilot so, and ask it to mark the item \"not found\"."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/find-what-is-missing-from-documentation.html#3-turn-the-gaps-into-questions", "t": "Find what is missing from documentation", "s": "3. Turn the gaps into questions", "x": "In the same chat, enter: In the docs-practice folder, create gaps.md: each item marked \"not found\", written as a question I can send to the team that owns Order Tracker. Add or change no other file. Copy In the list on the left, right-click gaps.md and select Open Preview . Check: gaps.md has a question for each item marked \"not found\". To use documents you were handed: save them in one folder with your own list of what you need, and open that folder. Send the same requests, except the one that creates practice files. Use your files' names and your application's name, and leave out the practice folder names. If it does not work: Help ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/review-a-change-for-go-or-no-go.html", "t": "Review a change request for go or no-go", "s": "", "x": "You end with: review.md , one row per change request: a suggested go, no-go or ask for more information, and why. You need: the copilot-practice folder open in VS Code. If another folder is open, on the File menu, select Open Recent , and then select copilot-practice . When: a change request needs your go or no-go, and the answer depends on checking what was submitted against what you require."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/review-a-change-for-go-or-no-go.html#1-have-copilot-create-practice-change-requests", "t": "Review a change request for go or no-go", "s": "1. Have Copilot create practice change requests", "x": "Copilot leaves items out of two of the change requests on purpose, so you know what it should find. If the control at the bottom of the chat box doesn't show Agent , select the control, and then select Agent . In the chat box, enter: In the copilot-practice folder, create a folder named change-practice. In it, create checklist.md: the four things a change must have before it is approved. The four are an owner, a time window, a rollback plan and a test result. Then create change-requests.md in the same folder, with three made-up change requests named CHG-201, CHG-202 and CHG-203. Give CHG-201 all four things. Leave the rollback plan out of CHG-202. Leave the owner and the test result out of CHG-203. Add or change no other file. Copy Check: change-practice holds checklist.md and change-requests.md ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/review-a-change-for-go-or-no-go.html#2-check-each-change-request-against-the-checklist", "t": "Review a change request for go or no-go", "s": "2. Check each change request against the checklist", "x": "A quote shows where a change request meets an item. \"Not stated\" gives Copilot a way to report a missing item instead of guessing. Enter: Read change-requests.md and checklist.md in the change-practice folder of the copilot-practice folder. For each change request and each checklist item, quote the words in the request that meet it. Where the request doesn't say, write \"not stated\". Don't guess. Don't change any file. Copy Check: CHG-201 meets all four. CHG-202's rollback plan is \"not stated\", and so are CHG-203's owner and test result. For CHG-201, find each quote in change-requests.md . If an item that was left out has a quote instead, or CHG-201 has an item \"not stated\", find the item in change-requests.md . A quote such as \"Rollback: none provided\" doesn't meet the item. Tell Copilot what you found, and ask it to correct the item."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/review-a-change-for-go-or-no-go.html#3-save-a-suggested-decision", "t": "Review a change request for go or no-go", "s": "3. Save a suggested decision", "x": "Copilot only suggests. The decision stays yours. In the same chat, enter: In the change-practice folder, create review.md: a table with one row per change request. In each row, give a suggested go, no-go, or ask for more information, and why. Add or change no other file. Copy In the list on the left, right-click review.md and select Open Preview . Check: CHG-201 is a go, and the other two are no-go or ask for more information, each with its missing items as the reason. To use your own change requests: save them in one folder with your checklist, and open that folder. Send the same requests, except the one that creates practice files. Use your files' names, and leave out the practice folder names. If it does not work: Help ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/organize-a-folder.html", "t": "Organize a folder", "s": "", "x": "You end with: a folder sorted into subfolders, and moves.md , a record of where each file was and where it went. You need: the copilot-practice folder open in VS Code. If another folder is open, on the File menu, select Open Recent , and then select copilot-practice . When: a folder grew without a plan, such as your desktop or a shared project folder."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/organize-a-folder.html#1-have-copilot-create-a-cluttered-folder", "t": "Organize a folder", "s": "1. Have Copilot create a cluttered folder", "x": "The folder gives you files to sort without touching your own. If the control at the bottom of the chat box doesn't show Agent , select the control, and then select Agent . In the chat box, enter: In the copilot-practice folder, create a folder named inbox. In it, create 12 short, made-up files: meeting notes, drafts of team emails, and ticket exports as .csv files. Put a date from August or September in each file's name. Add or change no other file. Copy Check: inbox holds 12 files. If it holds a different number, expect that number below."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/organize-a-folder.html#2-ask-for-a-plan-and-change-nothing-yet", "t": "Organize a folder", "s": "2. Ask for a plan, and change nothing yet", "x": "Reading the plan first lets you correct it before any file moves. Enter: List the files in the inbox folder of the copilot-practice folder, grouped by kind and by month. Propose subfolders inside inbox to sort them into. Don't change any file yet. Copy Check: the reply lists all 12 files and proposes subfolders, and inbox hasn't changed."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/organize-a-folder.html#3-change-the-plan-by-asking-again", "t": "Organize a folder", "s": "3. Change the plan by asking again", "x": "You don't have to accept the first plan. Asking again changes it. Enter: Change the plan so the email drafts go in a subfolder named emails. In the new plan, leave the .csv files where they are. Don't change any file yet. Copy Check: the new plan has an emails subfolder and leaves the .csv files in inbox ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/organize-a-folder.html#4-save-the-plan-then-move-the-files", "t": "Organize a folder", "s": "4. Save the plan, then move the files", "x": "Saving the plan first gives you a record of where everything was. Enter: In the inbox folder, create moves.md: one row per file, with where it is now and where it will go. Then move the files as moves.md says. Tell me any file the plan moves that didn't move. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if everything it creates or moves is inside inbox . Check: inbox holds the new subfolders, the .csv files and moves.md , and moves.md has a row for each of the 12 files. If you want the files back where they were moves.md records where each file was, so Copilot can put them back. Enter: Move every file in the inbox folder back to where moves.md says it was. Add or change no other file. Copy To use your own folder: make a copy of it, and open the copy. Send the same requests, except the one that creates practice files. Use the copy's name in place of inbox, and leave out the practice folder names. If it does not work: Help ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/change-many-files-at-once.html", "t": "Change many files at once", "s": "", "x": "You end with: updated copies of a folder of files, with the originals unchanged. You need: the copilot-practice folder open in VS Code. If another folder is open, on the File menu, select Open Recent , and then select copilot-practice . When: the same change is needed in many files, such as a new phone number, a renamed team or a replaced term."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/change-many-files-at-once.html#1-have-copilot-create-practice-notices", "t": "Change many files at once", "s": "1. Have Copilot create practice notices", "x": "Five of the notices give the same phone number, so you know how many files should change. If the control at the bottom of the chat box doesn't show Agent , select the control, and then select Agent . In the chat box, enter: In the copilot-practice folder, create a folder named notices. In it, write eight short, made-up notices to staff about office services. In five of them, give the service desk's phone number as 555-0100. Add or change no other file. Copy Check: notices holds eight files. If it holds a different number, expect that number below."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/change-many-files-at-once.html#2-ask-what-would-change-and-change-nothing-yet", "t": "Change many files at once", "s": "2. Ask what would change, and change nothing yet", "x": "Seeing which files a change touches lets you catch a wrong match before anything changes. Enter: In the notices folder of the copilot-practice folder, list each file that gives the phone number 555-0100, with the sentence it's in. Don't change any file yet. Copy Check: the reply lists five files. If it lists a different number of files, expect that number below."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/change-many-files-at-once.html#3-make-the-change-on-copies", "t": "Change many files at once", "s": "3. Make the change on copies", "x": "Working on copies leaves the originals as they were. Enter: In the copilot-practice folder, create a folder named notices-updated. Copy every file from the notices folder into it. In the copies, replace 555-0100 with 555-0199, and change nothing else in them. Tell me how many files you copied and how many you changed. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it copies files from notices and changes only files in notices-updated . Check: Copilot reports eight files copied and five changed, and notices-updated holds eight files. Open one of the five in notices-updated . It gives 555-0199. Open the same file in notices . It still gives 555-0100. To use your own folder: open the folder that holds it. Send the same requests, except the one that creates practice files. Use your folder's name in place of notices and your change in place of the phone numbers, and leave out the practice folder names. If it does not work: Help ."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/index.html", "t": "Skills", "s": "", "x": "In this training, you learn what a skill is and create one. A skill is a saved set of instructions for a task you repeat; Copilot follows them when you ask for that task."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/index.html#what-you-will-be-able-to-do", "t": "Skills", "s": "What you will be able to do", "x": "Say what a skill is, and how Copilot decides to use one. Create a skill, and have Copilot use it without naming it."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/index.html#before-you-start", "t": "Skills", "s": "Before you start", "x": "You have finished Getting started . VS Code is open, with the chat set to Agent ."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/index.html#the-path", "t": "Skills", "s": "The path", "x": "What is a skill? : what a skill is for, what one looks like, and how Copilot uses it. Create your first skill : ask for a meeting summary with every instruction typed out, have Copilot save them as a skill, and get the same summary by asking in one line."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/what-is-a-skill.html", "t": "What is a skill?", "s": "", "x": "A skill is a saved set of instructions for one kind of task. You write the instructions once, and Copilot follows them when you ask for that task, so you don't type them again."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/what-is-a-skill.html#why-use-a-skill", "t": "What is a skill?", "s": "Why use a skill", "x": "Some tasks you ask for again and again, and want done the same way each time: a weekly summary in the same layout the same checks on every new document Without a skill, you type the full instructions every time, and the result changes when your wording does. With a skill, you ask in a few words, and Copilot follows the saved instructions."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/what-is-a-skill.html#what-a-skill-looks-like", "t": "What is a skill?", "s": "What a skill looks like", "x": "A skill is a folder holding a Markdown file named SKILL.md . This one summarizes meeting notes: --- name: meeting-summary description: Summarize meeting notes for busy, non-technical readers. Use when asked to summarize meeting notes. --- Lead with the decisions. Then list the actions, each with its owner and due date. After each item, name the note it came from. The lines between the two --- marks are the skill's header: its name and its description. After the header come the skill's instructions."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/what-is-a-skill.html#how-copilot-uses-a-skill", "t": "What is a skill?", "s": "How Copilot uses a skill", "x": "Copilot checks the description of each skill you have. When your request matches a skill's description, such as \"summarize the meeting notes\", Copilot follows that skill's instructions. That's why a description says both what the skill does and when to use it. .dg-ink{fill:var(--ink,#1b1b1b)} .dg-ink2{fill:var(--ink2,#505050)} .dg-req{fill:var(--accent-soft,#eef4fa);stroke:var(--accent,#0065b3)} .dg-card{fill:var(--bg2,#f7f7f7);stroke:var(--ctl,#767676)} .dg-hit{fill:var(--bg2,#f7f7f7);stroke:var(--accent,#0065b3);stroke-width:2} .dg-dim{opacity:.45} .dg-arrow{stroke:var(--accent,#0065b3);stroke-width:2;fill:none} .dg-head{fill:var(--accent,#0065b3)} .dg-faint{stroke:var(--ctl,#767676);stroke-width:1.5;fill:none;stroke-dasharray:4 4} Your request “Summarize the meeting notes.” The description of each skill you have Check a new document Summarize meeting notes Make the weekly summary Copilot follows Its instructions Lead with the decisions. Then list the actions, each with its owner and due date. After each item, name the note it came from. You can also run a skill by name: type / in the chat box, and select the skill from the list."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/what-is-a-skill.html#where-a-skill-works", "t": "What is a skill?", "s": "Where a skill works", "x": "A skill saved in a folder works only while that folder is open in VS Code, and anyone who opens that folder has it. A skill saved in your personal skills folder works in every folder you open, for you only. Copilot saves a skill in either place when you ask."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/what-is-a-skill.html#skills-and-custom-instructions", "t": "What is a skill?", "s": "Skills and custom instructions", "x": "In the Getting started unit Give Copilot custom instructions for a folder , you saved rules for summaries as custom instructions . Copilot read them with every request while the folder was open. Instructions saved as a skill are read only when you ask for that task. That means they never change Copilot's answers to anything else, and you can keep one skill for each task you repeat. The home page's Which to use when shows where skills fit beside custom instructions, custom agents and connections."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/what-is-a-skill.html#what-you-learned", "t": "What is a skill?", "s": "What you learned", "x": "A skill is a folder with a SKILL.md file: a header that says what the skill does and when to use it, then the instructions. Copilot uses it when a request matches its description, or when you select it with / . Source: Use Agent Skills in VS Code , Microsoft."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/create-your-first-skill.html", "t": "Create your first skill", "s": "", "x": "A skill saves your instructions for a task, so you don't type them again. In this unit, you ask for a meeting summary with every instruction typed out, and have Copilot save those instructions as a skill. Then you get the same summary by asking in one line."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/create-your-first-skill.html#1-have-copilot-create-practice-meeting-notes", "t": "Create your first skill", "s": "1. Have Copilot create practice meeting notes", "x": "Copilot creates meeting notes to practice on, in a new folder for this training. In the chat box, enter: Create a new folder named skills-practice in the folder Windows uses as my Documents folder, which may be under OneDrive. If that folder already exists, don't change it; tell me instead. In it, create a folder named notes with three short, made-up notes from a weekly team meeting. Each note lists what was decided, and the actions with an owner and a due date. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it matches what you asked. On the File menu, select Open Folder . In the window that opens, go to Documents, the same Documents that File Explorer shows on the left. Select skills-practice , and then Select Folder . If VS Code asks whether you trust the authors of the files, select Yes, I trust the authors . If a bar at the top says the folder is in Restricted Mode instead, select Manage , and then Trust . Check: the list on the left shows SKILLS-PRACTICE , with notes in it, and a control at the bottom of the chat box says Agent . If no control shows Agent If no control there says Agent , or the controls show only icons, select the chat box, hold down Ctrl, and press the period key (.). The list of agents opens, with the selected agent marked. Select Agent ."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/create-your-first-skill.html#2-ask-for-a-meeting-summary-without-a-skill", "t": "Create your first skill", "s": "2. Ask for a meeting summary without a skill", "x": "To get a summary the way you want it, you type out every instruction. Enter: Summarize the meeting notes in the notes folder. Lead with the decisions. Then list the actions, each with its owner and due date. After each item, name the note it came from. Copy Check: the summary leads with the decisions, gives each action an owner and a due date, and names the note behind each item. Without a skill, you'd type all those instructions every time."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/create-your-first-skill.html#3-have-copilot-save-the-summary-instructions-as-a-skill", "t": "Create your first skill", "s": "3. Have Copilot save the summary instructions as a skill", "x": "Saved as a skill, these instructions are used when you ask for a meeting summary, without typing them again. Enter: Save the instructions from my last request as a skill named meeting-summary. Put it in .github/skills/meeting-summary/SKILL.md in the skills-practice folder. Use it whenever I ask to summarize meeting notes. If a skill with that name already exists, don't change it; tell me instead. Add or change no other file. Copy In the list on the left, expand .github until SKILL.md shows, and select it. Check: the file holds your instructions, and a description saying to use the skill when you ask to summarize meeting notes. Important: this skill is saved in the skills-practice folder, so it works only while that folder is open in VS Code. To have a skill work in every folder, ask Copilot to save it in your personal skills folder instead."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/create-your-first-skill.html#4-ask-for-the-summary-again-with-the-skill", "t": "Create your first skill", "s": "4. Ask for the summary again, with the skill", "x": "This time you ask in one line, and Copilot follows the skill. Select New Chat (+). Enter: Summarize the meeting notes in the notes folder. Copy Check: the summary leads with the decisions, gives each action an owner and a due date, and names the note behind each item, like the one you typed out in full. If the summary isn't laid out that way Copilot doesn't use a skill for every request that could match its description. Run it by name instead: type /meet , select meeting-summary , then type the notes folder and press Enter. To have Copilot find out why and fix the skill, send: Review the meeting-summary skill. Tell me why it wasn't used when I asked to summarize the meeting notes, and fix it. Copy"}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/create-your-first-skill.html#on-your-own-work", "t": "Create your first skill", "s": "On your own work", "x": "When you catch yourself typing the same instructions again, ask Copilot to save them as a skill, and say when to use it. To share a skill, give someone a copy of its folder."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/create-your-first-skill.html#what-you-did", "t": "Create your first skill", "s": "What you did", "x": "You asked for a summary with every instruction typed out, and had Copilot save those instructions as a skill. Then you got the same summary from a one-line request. Source: Use Agent Skills in VS Code , Microsoft."}, {"u": "agents/index.html", "t": "Custom agents", "s": "", "x": "A custom agent is Copilot set up for one role, such as reviewer or planner. It has its own instructions, the tools it may use and, if you choose, its AI model. In this training, you learn when to use one. Then you create a reviewer that can read files but not change them, and give its reports a fixed layout."}, {"u": "agents/index.html#what-you-will-be-able-to-do", "t": "Custom agents", "s": "What you will be able to do", "x": "Say what a custom agent is, and when to use one rather than custom instructions , a skill or just a request. Create a custom agent that can read files but not change them, and use it. Give a custom agent a fixed layout for its reports."}, {"u": "agents/index.html#before-you-start", "t": "Custom agents", "s": "Before you start", "x": "You have finished Getting started and Skills . VS Code is open, and a control at the bottom of the chat box shows Agent ."}, {"u": "agents/index.html#the-path", "t": "Custom agents", "s": "The path", "x": "What is a custom agent? : what a custom agent is for, what one looks like, and how it differs from custom instructions and a skill. Create your first custom agent : have Copilot create a reviewer whose tools only read, use it on practice procedures, and see that it can't change them. Make a custom agent's reports consistent : add a report layout to the reviewer's instructions, and get the same layout however you ask."}, {"u": "agents/index.html#after-the-path", "t": "Custom agents", "s": "After the path", "x": "Once you finish the path, you can try the practice recipes . Each is an optional new task that uses what this training taught. Do any of them, in any order."}, {"u": "agents/what-is-a-custom-agent.html", "t": "What is a custom agent?", "s": "", "x": "A custom agent is Copilot set up for one role, such as reviewer or planner. It has its own instructions, the tools it may use and, if you choose, its AI model. In this unit, you learn what a custom agent is and when to use one. There's nothing to do in VS Code yet."}, {"u": "agents/what-is-a-custom-agent.html#why-use-a-custom-agent", "t": "What is a custom agent?", "s": "Why use a custom agent", "x": "A skill holds one task, such as a weekly summary. A custom agent covers a role, for as long as you have it selected. When you want Copilot in the same role often, select a custom agent instead of describing the role in every request. Each one gets what its role needs: A reviewer holds your review checklist. A planner has only the tools for reading files, so it can work out a change but not make it. A procedure writer holds your template and house rules, and keeps every tool. Copilot does its work with tools: one tool reads a file, another changes a file, another runs a command . A custom agent's tools come with it: while it's selected, Copilot has only those tools. Custom instructions or a skill can only ask Copilot not to change a file. Copilot still has every tool, including the one that changes files. A custom agent can be given only some of the tools. Without the one that changes files, it can't change a file, even if a request asks it to. .dg-ink{fill:var(--ink,#1b1b1b)} .dg-ink2{fill:var(--ink2,#505050)} .dg-chip{fill:var(--bg2,#f7f7f7);stroke:var(--ctl,#767676)} .dg-gone{fill:none;stroke:var(--ctl,#767676);stroke-dasharray:5 4} .dg-strike{stroke:var(--ink2,#505050);stroke-width:1.5} .dg-div{stroke:var(--rule,#e6e6e6)} Asked not to change a file by a request, custom instructions or a skill Read files Change files Still has the tool. It can change the file. A custom agent given only the tools for reading files Read files Change files Doesn't have the tool. It can't change the file. In Create your first custom agent , you build a reviewer that has only the tools for reading files. It lists unclear steps in your procedures and leaves the procedures unchanged. A custom agent can also hand its work on. After the reviewer replies, a button can pass its findings to Agent to fix. Agent is the agent you've used so far. The practice recipe Hand a review to Agent to fix shows how to add that button."}, {"u": "agents/what-is-a-custom-agent.html#what-a-custom-agent-looks-like", "t": "What is a custom agent?", "s": "What a custom agent looks like", "x": "A custom agent is a Markdown file whose name ends in .agent.md . You don't type one yourself: when you ask, Copilot writes the whole file. This one, procedure-reviewer.agent.md , checks procedures: --- name: Procedure reviewer description: Check a procedure for unclear or missing steps, without changing it. tools: ['read', 'search'] --- Read the procedure I name. List each step that is unclear or missing, and say why. The header, between the two --- marks, gives the agent's name, what it does and its tools. read and search let it read and search the files in the folder you have open. edit , the tool that changes files, isn't listed. The agent's instructions follow the header."}, {"u": "agents/what-is-a-custom-agent.html#how-you-select-a-custom-agent", "t": "What is a custom agent?", "s": "How you select a custom agent", "x": "A custom agent you create is added to the list that opens from the control showing Agent . The list may show other agents too; you can leave them alone. While a custom agent is selected, the control shows its name."}, {"u": "agents/what-is-a-custom-agent.html#custom-instructions-a-skill-or-a-custom-agent", "t": "What is a custom agent?", "s": "Custom instructions, a skill or a custom agent?", "x": "To have Copilot Use Copilot uses it Follow the same rules in every request in a folder Custom instructions With every request while the folder is open Do a task you repeat the same way each time A skill When a request matches its description, or you select it after typing / Work in one role, such as reviewer or planner, for a whole chat A custom agent While you have it selected in the chat box For something you need only once, say it in the request. Often more than one would work: choose the smallest change that does the job. The home page's Which to use when compares these with connections too. Which would you use for each of these? Decide, and then select Show the answer . a. Everything Copilot writes in a folder should spell out abbreviations. b. Each week, you ask Copilot to check the new procedures against a style guide and fix their wording to match. c. Each new procedure needs checking for missing steps. It must stay unchanged while it's checked, even if a request asks Copilot to fix it. d. You write new procedures several times a week. In each of those chats, you want Copilot to work as a procedure writer, from your template and house rules. e. One procedure needs checking for missing steps, just this once. Show the answer Check your answer: a. Custom instructions. It's a rule for every request in the folder. b. A skill. It's one task you repeat the same way. A custom agent working as an editor would also do it, but that's a bigger change than the task needs. c. A custom agent. Each procedure must stay unchanged even if a request asks for a fix. A skill can only ask; a reviewer without the tool that changes files can't change the procedure. d. A custom agent, or a skill. For a whole chat in one role, a custom agent: you select it once. A skill also works if you ask for one procedure at a time. e. None of them. Say it in the request."}, {"u": "agents/what-is-a-custom-agent.html#what-you-learned", "t": "What is a custom agent?", "s": "What you learned", "x": "A custom agent is Copilot set up for one role. It's a .agent.md file: a header, then its instructions. The header gives its name, a description, its tools and, if you choose, its AI model. You select it in the chat box. Use one for a role you need often. Its tools come with it, every time you select it. Source: Custom agents in VS Code and Understand agent customization , Microsoft."}, {"u": "agents/create-your-first-custom-agent.html", "t": "Create your first custom agent", "s": "", "x": "A reviewer that can only read files can check your documents and leave every one as written. In this unit, you have Copilot create practice procedures and a Procedure reviewer custom agent . You use it to check the procedures, and then ask it to change one."}, {"u": "agents/create-your-first-custom-agent.html#1-have-copilot-create-practice-procedures", "t": "Create your first custom agent", "s": "1. Have Copilot create practice procedures", "x": "Copilot creates procedures to practice on, in a new folder. In the chat box, enter: Create a new folder named agents-practice in the folder Windows uses as my Documents folder, which may be under OneDrive. If agents-practice already exists, don't change it; tell me instead. In it, create a folder named procedures with three short, made-up office procedures. Give each procedure numbered steps, with one unclear step and one step left out. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it matches what you asked. On the File menu, select Open Folder . In the window that opens, go to Documents, the same Documents that File Explorer shows on the left. Select agents-practice , and then Select Folder . If VS Code asks whether you trust the authors of the files, select Yes, I trust the authors . If a bar at the top says the folder is in Restricted Mode, select Manage , and then Trust . Check: the list on the left shows AGENTS-PRACTICE , with procedures in it, and a control at the bottom of the chat box shows Agent . If no control shows Agent If no control there says Agent , or the controls show only icons, select the chat box, hold down Ctrl, and press the period key (.). The list of agents opens, with the selected agent marked. Select Agent ."}, {"u": "agents/create-your-first-custom-agent.html#2-have-copilot-create-the-procedure-reviewer", "t": "Create your first custom agent", "s": "2. Have Copilot create the Procedure reviewer", "x": "You describe the role, and Copilot writes the custom agent's file. Enter: In the agents-practice folder, create the custom agent file .github/agents/procedure-reviewer.agent.md. In its header, set the name to Procedure reviewer. Set the tools in its header to read and search only. Set its description to: Check procedures for unclear or missing steps, without changing them. In its instructions, have it quote each unclear step and say why, and name each missing step. In its instructions, also have it give the file name of the procedure for each step. If that file already exists, don't change it; tell me instead. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it creates only that file. In the list on the left, expand .github until procedure-reviewer.agent.md shows, and select it. Check: the header's name line says Procedure reviewer, and its tools line lists read and search only. edit , the tool that changes files, isn't there. Important: this custom agent is saved in the agents-practice folder, so it works only while that folder is open in VS Code. To have a custom agent in every folder, ask Copilot to save it in your personal agents folder instead."}, {"u": "agents/create-your-first-custom-agent.html#3-select-the-procedure-reviewer", "t": "Create your first custom agent", "s": "3. Select the Procedure reviewer", "x": "While it's selected, Copilot works in its role, with only its tools. At the top of the chat, select New Chat (+). At the bottom of the chat box, select the control that shows Agent , and then select Procedure reviewer . Check: the control shows Procedure reviewer . If Procedure reviewer isn't in the list See A custom agent isn't in the list of agents ."}, {"u": "agents/create-your-first-custom-agent.html#4-have-the-procedure-reviewer-check-the-procedures", "t": "Create your first custom agent", "s": "4. Have the Procedure reviewer check the procedures", "x": "You ask in one line; its instructions say how to report. Enter: Check the procedures in the procedures folder. Copy Check: the reply quotes unclear steps and names missing ones, each with the file name of its procedure. In the list on the left, expand procedures , and select the file the reply names for one quoted step. Find that step in it."}, {"u": "agents/create-your-first-custom-agent.html#5-ask-the-procedure-reviewer-to-change-a-procedure", "t": "Create your first custom agent", "s": "5. Ask the Procedure reviewer to change a procedure", "x": "The reviewer has no tool to make the change you ask for. Enter: Rewrite the unclear steps in the procedures so they're clear. Copy If a permission request to change a file appears anyway, select Skip . Check: Copilot says it can't change the files, or shows new wording in the chat only. Open the procedure file you opened to check the reply: the step you found there is unchanged."}, {"u": "agents/create-your-first-custom-agent.html#on-your-own-work", "t": "Create your first custom agent", "s": "On your own work", "x": "For a kind of review you do often, ask Copilot to create a custom agent for it. Tell Copilot to use your checklist as its instructions, and to give it only the tools that read files."}, {"u": "agents/create-your-first-custom-agent.html#what-you-did", "t": "Create your first custom agent", "s": "What you did", "x": "You had Copilot create a custom agent that can only read files. You selected it, used it to check the procedures, and saw that it couldn't change them. Source: Custom agents in VS Code , Microsoft."}, {"u": "agents/make-its-reports-consistent.html", "t": "Make a custom agent's reports consistent", "s": "", "x": "A report laid out the same way every time is quicker to read and to check. In this unit, you have Copilot add a report layout to the Procedure reviewer's instructions. Then you ask for two reviews in different words and get the same layout. Start with the agents-practice folder open, as Create your first custom agent left it."}, {"u": "agents/make-its-reports-consistent.html#1-have-copilot-add-a-report-layout-to-the-procedure-reviewer", "t": "Make a custom agent's reports consistent", "s": "1. Have Copilot add a report layout to the Procedure reviewer", "x": "The reviewer can't change its own file, so you use Agent . At the top of the chat, select New Chat (+). At the bottom of the chat box, select the control that shows Procedure reviewer , and then select Agent . Enter: In the agents-practice folder, open .github/agents/procedure-reviewer.agent.md. At the end of its instructions, add this report layout: - A heading \"Procedures checked\", then the file name of each procedure checked. - A heading \"Unclear steps\", then a table with the columns Procedure, Step and Why. - A heading \"Missing steps\", then one line for each missing step, naming its procedure, or \"None\" if there are none. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it changes only that file. In the list on the left, select procedure-reviewer.agent.md . Check: the instructions end with the three headings in that order, and the tools line still lists read and search only."}, {"u": "agents/make-its-reports-consistent.html#2-have-the-procedure-reviewer-check-the-procedures", "t": "Make a custom agent's reports consistent", "s": "2. Have the Procedure reviewer check the procedures", "x": "A custom agent 's instructions apply to every request while it's selected. Select New Chat (+). At the bottom of the chat box, select the control that shows Agent , and then select Procedure reviewer . Enter: Check the procedures in the procedures folder. Copy Check: the reply has the headings Procedures checked, Unclear steps and Missing steps, in that order, with a table of Procedure, Step and Why under Unclear steps."}, {"u": "agents/make-its-reports-consistent.html#3-ask-the-procedure-reviewer-in-other-words-and-compare-the-layout", "t": "Make a custom agent's reports consistent", "s": "3. Ask the Procedure reviewer in other words, and compare the layout", "x": "The layout comes from the custom agent, not your wording. Select New Chat (+), and select Procedure reviewer again if the control doesn't show it. Enter: Which steps in the procedures folder would confuse someone doing them for the first time? Copy Check: the reply has the same three headings in the same order, and the same table columns. Open the file named in one table row, and find the step it quotes. If the layout differs Copilot may not have used the custom agent's instructions. Check that the control shows Procedure reviewer . To have Copilot find out why and fix the file, select the control, then Agent , and enter: Review .github/agents/procedure-reviewer.agent.md in the agents-practice folder. Tell me why the Procedure reviewer didn't use its report layout. Fix the file so it does. Add or change no other file. Copy"}, {"u": "agents/make-its-reports-consistent.html#on-your-own-work", "t": "Make a custom agent's reports consistent", "s": "On your own work", "x": "When you want a custom agent's reports laid out the same way each time, ask Copilot to add the layout to its instructions. Say the headings, in order, and what goes under each."}, {"u": "agents/make-its-reports-consistent.html#what-you-did", "t": "Make a custom agent's reports consistent", "s": "What you did", "x": "You had Copilot add a report layout to a custom agent, and got that layout from two requests worded differently. To go back to Agent , select the control at the bottom of the chat box, and then select Agent . Source: Custom agents in VS Code , Microsoft."}, {"u": "agents/how-to/index.html", "t": "Practice recipes", "s": "", "x": "This recipe is optional practice for after you finish Custom agents. It's a new task that uses what the training taught. Kind of work Recipe You end with One agent finds problems, another fixes them Hand a review to Agent to fix A Procedure reviewer whose reply ends with a button that hands what it found to Agent to fix"}, {"u": "agents/how-to/hand-a-review-to-agent.html", "t": "Hand a review to Agent to fix", "s": "", "x": "You end with: a Procedure reviewer that shows a button after each review. The button hands what the reviewer found to Agent to fix. You also end with practice procedures whose unclear steps Agent fixed. You need: the agents-practice folder open in VS Code, with the Procedure reviewer and the procedures folder in it. If another folder is open, on the File menu, select Open Recent , and then select agents-practice . When: a second agent works from what a first agent produced, and you want to read that before the second agent starts. A handoff is a button that a custom agent shows after its reply. Selecting it switches to another agent and fills in a request for that agent."}, {"u": "agents/how-to/hand-a-review-to-agent.html#1-have-copilot-add-a-handoff-to-the-procedure-reviewer", "t": "Hand a review to Agent to fix", "s": "1. Have Copilot add a handoff to the Procedure reviewer", "x": "Copilot adds the handoff to the reviewer's file. With send set to false, the request the button fills in waits in the chat box until you send it. Select New Chat (+). If the control at the bottom of the chat box doesn't show Agent , select the control, and then select Agent . Enter: In the agents-practice folder, open .github/agents/procedure-reviewer.agent.md. In its header, add a handoffs list with one handoff. Set the handoff's label to: Fix the unclear steps Set the handoff's agent to agent, in lowercase. Set the handoff's prompt to: Rewrite the unclear steps listed above so they're clear. Add or change no other file. Set the handoff's send to false. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it changes only that file. In the list on the left, select procedure-reviewer.agent.md . Check: the header has label: Fix the unclear steps and agent: agent , and the tools line still lists read and search only."}, {"u": "agents/how-to/hand-a-review-to-agent.html#2-have-the-procedure-reviewer-check-the-procedures", "t": "Hand a review to Agent to fix", "s": "2. Have the Procedure reviewer check the procedures", "x": "Select New Chat (+). Select the control that shows Agent , and then select Procedure reviewer . Enter: Check the procedures in the procedures folder. Copy Check: when the reply finishes, a button that says Fix the unclear steps shows below it, under Proceed from Procedure reviewer . If no button appears Select the control that shows Procedure reviewer , select Agent , and enter: Rewrite the unclear steps listed above so they're clear. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it changes only files in the procedures folder. Then do the Check under \"Hand the review to Agent\"."}, {"u": "agents/how-to/hand-a-review-to-agent.html#3-hand-the-review-to-agent", "t": "Hand a review to Agent to fix", "s": "3. Hand the review to Agent", "x": "Select Fix the unclear steps . The control now shows Agent . The chat box holds the handoff's request. Read the request, and then press Enter to send it. If a permission request appears, allow it if it changes only files in the procedures folder. Check: pick one unclear step in the reviewer's reply. Open the procedure it names, and find that step. Its wording has changed, and it reads more clearly than the quote. If it does not work: Help ."}, {"u": "connecting/index.html", "t": "Connecting your tools", "s": "", "x": "A connection lets Copilot get information that isn't in your files, from another service such as GitHub. In this training, you connect Copilot to GitHub, and use the connection to look up a public project on GitHub."}, {"u": "connecting/index.html#what-you-will-be-able-to-do", "t": "Connecting your tools", "s": "What you will be able to do", "x": "Say what a connection gives Copilot, and how you can tell when Copilot uses one. Add a connection to a folder and use it in a request."}, {"u": "connecting/index.html#before-you-start", "t": "Connecting your tools", "s": "Before you start", "x": "You have finished Getting started . VS Code is open, and a control at the bottom of the chat box shows Agent ."}, {"u": "connecting/index.html#the-path", "t": "Connecting your tools", "s": "The path", "x": "Connect Copilot to GitHub : add a read-only connection to GitHub in a practice folder, and see Copilot use it to answer a question about a public project on GitHub."}, {"u": "connecting/connect-to-github.html", "t": "Connect Copilot to GitHub", "s": "", "x": "Copilot can use a connection to get information that isn't in your files. In this unit, you connect Copilot to GitHub in a practice folder. You ask a question about a public project on GitHub and see Copilot look it up there."}, {"u": "connecting/connect-to-github.html#what-a-connection-gives-copilot", "t": "Connect Copilot to GitHub", "s": "What a connection gives Copilot", "x": "Copilot does its work with tools . A tool is one action it can take, such as reading a file. A connection adds tools from another service. VS Code calls a connection an MCP server . When a request needs one of those tools, Copilot uses it, and the chat shows a line for each tool it ran. The connection in this unit is GitHub's read-only one. Its tools only look things up on GitHub and can't change anything there. The connection signs in to GitHub with your work GitHub account, the one you use for Copilot. You sign in once, when VS Code asks. You don't set up anything on GitHub first."}, {"u": "connecting/connect-to-github.html#1-have-copilot-create-a-practice-folder", "t": "Connect Copilot to GitHub", "s": "1. Have Copilot create a practice folder", "x": "Copilot creates an empty folder to hold the connection while you practice. In the chat box, enter: Create a new folder named connections-practice in the folder Windows uses as my Documents folder, which may be under OneDrive. If connections-practice already exists, don't change it; tell me instead. Leave the new folder empty. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it matches what you asked. On the File menu, select Open Folder . In the window that opens, go to Documents, the same Documents that File Explorer shows on the left. Select connections-practice , and then Select Folder . If VS Code asks whether you trust the authors of the files, select Yes, I trust the authors . If a bar at the top says the folder is in Restricted Mode, select Manage , and then Trust . Check: the list on the left shows CONNECTIONS-PRACTICE , and a control at the bottom of the chat box shows Agent . If no control shows Agent If no control there shows Agent , or the controls show only icons, select the chat box, hold down Ctrl, and press the period key (.). The list of agents opens, with the selected agent marked. Select Agent ."}, {"u": "connecting/connect-to-github.html#2-have-copilot-add-the-github-connection", "t": "Connect Copilot to GitHub", "s": "2. Have Copilot add the GitHub connection", "x": "A connection is a few lines in a file named .mcp.json . Copilot writes them. Enter: In the connections-practice folder, create a file named .mcp.json. In it, add one MCP server named github, under mcpServers. Set its type to http. Set its url to https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/readonly. If .mcp.json already exists, don't change it; tell me instead. Add or change no other file. Copy If a permission request appears, allow it if it creates only that file. In the list on the left, select .mcp.json . If a line of small text above github in the file says Start , select it. This starts the connection. If a message says an MCP server wants to authenticate to GitHub, select Allow . If VS Code then asks you to choose an account or sign in, choose your work GitHub account. Wait until the line above github says Running . It can take a few seconds. Check: the line above github says Running . If the line above github doesn't say Running If it says Start , select it, and select Allow if asked. If there's no line above github at all, or it still doesn't say Running , see When you're stuck, ask Copilot . If you don't sign in to GitHub at github.com If the GitHub sign-in page you use for Copilot isn't on github.com, this connection's address is different. See GitHub Enterprise in GitHub's documentation. Important: this connection is saved in the connections-practice folder, so it works only while that folder is open in VS Code. To use it in another folder, have Copilot add it there."}, {"u": "connecting/connect-to-github.html#3-ask-a-question-that-uses-the-connection", "t": "Connect Copilot to GitHub", "s": "3. Ask a question that uses the connection", "x": "Copilot uses the connection to look up the answer on GitHub, and the chat shows it did. GitHub calls a project a repository, and each saved change to it a commit. At the top of the chat, select New Chat (+). Enter: Use the GitHub connection to answer this question. List the commits of the public octocat/Hello-World repository on GitHub. For the newest commit, tell me its date, its author and its message. Copy If a message says an MCP server wants to authenticate to GitHub, select Allow , and choose your work GitHub account. If a permission request appears, allow it if it names github. Check: the reply shows what Copilot did, one line for each tool it ran. If those lines are folded under one line, select it to show them. One line says Ran List commits , the connection's tool for listing commits. The answer says the newest commit was made in March 2012 by The Octocat, with a message that starts \"Merge pull request #6\". If no line says Ran List commits Select .mcp.json in the list on the left. If the line above github doesn't say Running , select Start there, select Allow if asked, and wait until it says Running . Then select New Chat (+) and enter the request again. If it still doesn't work, see When you're stuck, ask Copilot ."}, {"u": "connecting/connect-to-github.html#on-your-own-work", "t": "Connect Copilot to GitHub", "s": "On your own work", "x": "When a request needs information from another service, ask Copilot whether a connection exists for that service. Copilot sends the service whatever it asks the service to look up. In this unit, that went to GitHub. Before you add a connection, make sure you're allowed to send that service what your requests will look up. Add the connection to the folder you work in. It lives in that folder's .mcp.json . To remove it, have Copilot delete it from that file."}, {"u": "connecting/connect-to-github.html#what-you-did", "t": "Connect Copilot to GitHub", "s": "What you did", "x": "You added GitHub's read-only connection in a practice folder and saw Copilot use it to look up a public repository. Source: Add and manage MCP servers in VS Code , Microsoft; Remote GitHub MCP Server , GitHub."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#when-you-re-stuck-ask-copilot", "t": "Help", "s": "When you're stuck, ask Copilot", "x": "Copilot can see your chat, so it can tell you what happened and what to try. On a step: most steps have Stuck? Copy a question for Copilot under them. Select it and paste into the chat box. The question asks Copilot to explain the step without doing it for you. A request that didn't do what you expected: Ask Copilot: My last request didn't do what I expected. Tell me what you did, what went wrong, and what I should try next. Don't change anything. Copy An error in the chat: Ask Copilot: Explain the last error in this chat in plain words. Tell me the one thing to do next. Don't change anything. Copy What Copilot has done so far: Ask Copilot: List every file you created, changed or deleted in this chat. Copy"}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#getting-set-up", "t": "Help", "s": "Getting set up", "x": ""}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#vs-code-doesn-t-open", "t": "Help", "s": "VS Code doesn't open", "x": "If pressing the Windows key and typing code finds nothing, VS Code isn't installed. Install it from your company's software portal, then try again."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#the-chat-asks-you-to-sign-in-or-doesn-t-answer", "t": "Help", "s": "The chat asks you to sign in, or doesn't answer", "x": "Copilot is built into VS Code; there's nothing to install. In order: At the bottom of the VS Code window, hover over the Copilot icon. If it offers Use AI Features or Sign in to use Copilot , select it and sign in with your work GitHub account. Update VS Code: install the newest version your company's software portal offers. If a reply says you've sent too many requests, wait a few minutes and send it again. If it says you've used your monthly allowance, select the Copilot icon at the bottom of the window to see your usage; the allowance resets each month. Signed in with the wrong account Select the Accounts icon at the bottom of the bar on the far left of the window, then Sign out . Then sign in again from the Copilot icon with your work GitHub account."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#the-chat-isn-t-there", "t": "Help", "s": "The chat isn't there", "x": "On the View menu, select Chat . Or press Ctrl+Alt+I."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#copilot-answers-but-doesn-t-create-or-change-files", "t": "Help", "s": "Copilot answers but doesn't create or change files", "x": "Copilot may be set to another agent. Select the chat box, hold down Ctrl, and press the period key (.). The list of agents opens, with the selected agent marked. Select Agent . If Agent is already selected, Copilot may be set only to plan the work. A control below the chat box, not inside it, then shows Plan . In a narrow chat, it shows only an icon. Select Plan , and then select Interactive in the list that opens. With Interactive , Copilot does the work instead of only planning it."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#while-copilot-is-working", "t": "Help", "s": "While Copilot is working", "x": ""}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#nothing-seems-to-happen-or-it-s-taking-a-long-time", "t": "Help", "s": "Nothing seems to happen, or it's taking a long time", "x": "A moving indicator in the chat means Copilot is still working. Some requests take a few minutes. To change course, type what you want instead and press Enter. Copilot finishes its current action, and then takes your new message."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#a-panel-full-of-text-opened-at-the-bottom-of-the-window", "t": "Help", "s": "A panel full of text opened at the bottom of the window", "x": "That's the terminal , where the commands Copilot runs show their output. You don't need to type there."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#a-permission-request-you-don-t-understand", "t": "Help", "s": "A permission request you don't understand", "x": "If you can't tell whether the command matches what you asked for, select Skip . Nothing runs. Then send: Ask Copilot: What command did you want to run, and which files and folders would it have changed? Don't run it. Copy More on reading one: Reading a permission request ."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#copilot-asks-you-a-question-or-offers-choices", "t": "Help", "s": "Copilot asks you a question, or offers choices", "x": "Answer in the chat box, as you would answer a person."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#copilot-says-it-can-t-read-a-file", "t": "Help", "s": "Copilot says it can't read a file", "x": "Word, Excel and PDF files aren't plain text, so Copilot may ask to run a small command to read them. If the command only reads the file you named, allow it. If it installs or downloads anything, select Skip . If Copilot still can't read the file, save it as plain text (for an Excel file, choose CSV in Save As) and ask again."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#the-reply-is-too-long-or-too-technical", "t": "Help", "s": "The reply is too long or too technical", "x": "Say so in a follow-up : Explain that in plain words, in five lines or fewer. Copy"}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#when-the-result-is-wrong", "t": "Help", "s": "When the result is wrong", "x": ""}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#copilot-says-something-you-can-t-find-in-the-file", "t": "Help", "s": "Copilot says something you can't find in the file", "x": "Ask Copilot: Name the file and line numbers where that is, and quote those lines. Copy If the quote isn't in the file, send: That isn't in the file. Look again, and quote only what's in the file. Copy"}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#copilot-changed-something-you-didn-t-want-changed", "t": "Help", "s": "Copilot changed something you didn't want changed", "x": "Hover over the request that made the change and select Restore Checkpoint , as in Undo the table . Restore Checkpoint puts the files back as they were before that request, and removes that request and every later one from the chat. It doesn't reverse a command already run, such as a move or a delete. For those, ask: List every file you moved or deleted in this chat, and put each one back where it was. If you can't restore one exactly, say so instead of recreating it. Change nothing else. Copy"}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#copilot-worked-in-the-wrong-folder", "t": "Help", "s": "Copilot worked in the wrong folder", "x": "If the files it changed are in the folder you have open, select Restore Checkpoint on that request. For files anywhere else, use the request in Copilot changed something you didn't want changed . Then ask again, and name the folder in the request."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#copilot-has-forgotten-what-you-did", "t": "Help", "s": "Copilot has forgotten what you did", "x": "A new chat starts empty, but the files stay. Name the file in your request, for example: Read actions.md in the copilot-practice folder and continue from there. Copy"}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#the-chat-is-going-in-circles", "t": "Help", "s": "The chat is going in circles", "x": "Select New Chat (+) at the top of the chat. Then ask again in one request: which folder, what to read, what to make, and where to put it."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#skills-and-custom-agents", "t": "Help", "s": "Skills and custom agents", "x": ""}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#a-skill-isn-t-used-or-does-the-wrong-thing", "t": "Help", "s": "A skill isn't used, or does the wrong thing", "x": "Tell Copilot what you asked and what happened, and ask it to review the skill and fix it. A skill saved in a folder works only while that folder is open; one in your personal skills folder works everywhere."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#a-custom-agent-isn-t-in-the-list-of-agents", "t": "Help", "s": "A custom agent isn't in the list of agents", "x": "Ask Copilot: Ask Copilot: List the custom agent files in the .github/agents folder of the folder open in VS Code. Also list those in my personal agents folder: the agents folder inside .copilot in my user folder. For each, check where it is saved, that its header is complete, and that nothing in it hides it from the list. Tell me which of them VS Code can't list, and why. Don't change any file. Copy Then ask Copilot to fix what it found."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#questions", "t": "Help", "s": "Questions", "x": ""}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#do-i-need-to-write-code", "t": "Help", "s": "Do I need to write code?", "x": "No. You ask in plain words, and Copilot reads and writes the files."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#is-this-the-same-as-copilot-in-my-office-suite", "t": "Help", "s": "Is this the same as Copilot in my office suite?", "x": "No. That one reaches across the shared content at your work: mail, chats, meetings and shared files. This one works on a folder of files on your computer and can act on them."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#can-copilot-reach-other-systems-such-as-a-ticketing-system-directly", "t": "Help", "s": "Can Copilot reach other systems, such as a ticketing system, directly?", "x": "Only through a connection to that system; VS Code calls these MCP servers . Without one, export the data to a file and ask Copilot about the file."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#how-do-i-put-the-chat-on-the-other-side-of-the-window", "t": "Help", "s": "How do I put the chat on the other side of the window?", "x": "Ask Copilot: How do I put the chat on the other side of the VS Code window? Tell me the steps; don't change anything. Copy"}, {"u": "glossary.html", "t": "Glossary", "s": "", "x": "A word not here? Ask Copilot what it means."}, {"u": "glossary.html#on-your-screen", "t": "Glossary", "s": "On your screen", "x": "Word Meaning here Agent Copilot with a set of instructions and tools. You select one from the control at the bottom of the chat box. The one named Agent can create and change files and run commands Chat The panel where you type requests to Copilot. The chat box is the field at its bottom Explorer The list on the left of VS Code showing the open folder's files. If it's missing, on the View menu, select Explorer Permission request Allow and Skip buttons in a reply, asking whether Copilot may run a command. How to read one: Reading a permission request Preview The formatted view of a Markdown file. In the list on the left, right-click the file and select Open Preview Terminal The panel at the bottom of VS Code where the commands Copilot runs show their output VS Code Visual Studio Code, the program you work in"}, {"u": "glossary.html#working-with-copilot", "t": "Glossary", "s": "Working with Copilot", "x": "Word Meaning here Checkpoint Your files as they were before a request. Restore Checkpoint on a request puts them back Command An instruction Copilot runs on your computer, such as creating a folder. It asks before running one that could change something Connection What lets Copilot read or act on another service, such as GitHub. VS Code calls it an MCP server Copilot GitHub Copilot in VS Code, which this training is about. Not Copilot in your office suite Custom agent An agent set up for one role, with its own instructions, the tools it may use and, if you choose, its AI model. You select it in the chat box Custom instructions Notes Copilot reads before every request in a folder: what your work is, who it's for and how you want it done. Kept in .github/copilot-instructions.md Follow-up A request that builds on the last one in the same chat, so it can be short Markdown Plain text with simple marks for headings and lists, in a file ending .md . Its preview shows it formatted MCP server A connection that lets Copilot read or act on another service directly, instead of on a file exported from it New Chat A fresh conversation, started with New Chat (+) at the top of the chat. It doesn't remember earlier chats; the files stay Request What you type or paste into the chat to ask Copilot for something Skill Saved instructions for one kind of task, which Copilot follows when you ask for that task. A skill in a folder works while that folder is open; one in your personal skills folder works in every folder Subagent An agent Copilot hands part of a job to; it does that part and reports back Tool One action Copilot can take, such as reading a file, changing a file or running a command. An agent can do only what its tools allow"}, {"u": "CHANGELOG.html#1-3-2026-10-07", "t": "What's new", "s": "1.3 - 2026-10-07", "x": "Home page: a new \"Which to use when\" table. It shows when to use custom instructions , a skill , a custom agent or a connection, and which training teaches each. Custom agents, \"What is a custom agent?\": a custom agent is set up for a role, such as reviewer, planner or writer. Taking a tool away is one reason to use one, not the only one. The exercise has a fifth case."}, {"u": "CHANGELOG.html#1-2-2026-10-05", "t": "What's new", "s": "1.2 - 2026-10-05", "x": "Custom agents, \"What is a custom agent?\": the answer to the four cases lists each case on its own line."}, {"u": "CHANGELOG.html#1-1-2026-10-05", "t": "What's new", "s": "1.1 - 2026-10-05", "x": "Help, \"Getting set up\": install and update VS Code from your company's software portal, and sign in with your work GitHub account. In the menu on the left, Practice recipes opens to list each recipe, on every page of the training."}, {"u": "CHANGELOG.html#1-0-2026-10-03", "t": "What's new", "s": "1.0 - 2026-10-03", "x": "The first release: Getting started, Skills, Custom agents and Connecting your tools."}];
